<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemy I Soter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 367–282 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ptolemy I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptolemy I Soter was a Macedonian general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> who became ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after Alexander's death. He took the royal title in 305 BCE, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>. During the wars of the successors he was at times an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter.jpg" alt="Ptolemy I Soter" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy_I_Soter_Louvre_Ma849_A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ptolemy was the son of Lagus, a Macedonian nobleman from Eordaea, and Arsinoe, who according to Satyrus the Peripatetic descended from Alexander I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. Some ancient sources claim that Lagus was an illegitimate son of Philip II, which would have made Ptolemy a half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, but this is rejected as a later myth fabricated to glorify the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic dynasty</a>. Ptolemy served with Alexander from his first campaigns and was one of his seven bodyguards. He also wrote an eyewitness history of Alexander's campaigns, which is lost but largely survives in paraphrase in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>'s Anabasis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Alexander died in 323 BCE, the Partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> made Ptolemy satrap of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He seized Alexander's body on its way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, buried it at Memphis, and later had it moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. In 321 BCE Perdiccas invaded Egypt and was murdered by his own officers, and Ptolemy declined the offer of the regency. In the long wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a> he aimed first to hold Egypt, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, including Judea. He fought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> against Demetrius at Gaza in 312 BCE. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> and Demetrius took the title of king, he did the same, and the ownership of southern Syria caused recurring warfare between the Seleucid and Ptolemaic dynasties for the next hundred years.</p><p>Ptolemy made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> a closed monetary zone, requiring foreign coins to be exchanged at its borders. He commanded the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria">Lighthouse of Alexandria</a>. He made his son Ptolemy II his co-regent in 285 BCE, and died in 282 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ptolemy left what the sources describe as a compact, well-ordered realm after forty years of war. His reputation for good nature and generosity attached Macedonian and Greek soldiers to his service, and he was a patron of letters. His last wife was Berenice I, and her son succeeded him as Ptolemy II, while his sons by Eurydice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-keraunos">Ptolemy Keraunos</a> and Meleager, ruled in turn as kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. The dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> until the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> in 30 BCE. According to Proclus, Ptolemy sponsored the mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>, and told him that he found the Elements too difficult; Euclid replied that there is no royal road to geometry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ptolemy I Soter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ptolemaic Kingdom was founded by <b>Ptolemy I Soter</b>, one of the generals of Alexander the Great, after Alexander’s empire broke up following his death in 323 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Demetrius Poliorcetes besieged Rhodes for about a year to force it to abandon its neutrality and its friendship with <b>Ptolemy I</b>. Demetrius used huge siege engines, including the armoured tower known as the helepolis, but the Rhodians, supplied by Ptolemy, held out until a settlement left them free.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…royal hypaspists under Alexander and the Companion cavalry under the regent Perdiccas, was appointed satrap of Babylonia in 320 BCE but was driven out by the dominant Diadochos Antigonus I Monophthalmus in 316 BCE, and spent several years in exile at the court of <b>Ptolemy I</b> in Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ptolemy I Soter</b>, a general of Alexander the Great, took the titles of basileus and pharaoh in 305 BCE, and every male king of his line was named Ptolemy.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>The monarchy was ended under <b>Ptolemy I</b> c. 312 BCE, and Amathus continued as a city under the Ptolemaic Empire and Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-amathus">Kingdom of Amathus</a></li><li><blockquote>Magas, son of <b>Ptolemy I's</b> queen Berenice I by an earlier marriage, became governor of Cyrene around 300 BCE after a revolt there was suppressed.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cyrene-under-magas">Kingdom of Cyrene under Magas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Macedonia. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=-312" aria-label="Alexandria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5322 1027 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ptolemy I Soter"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5772 1297h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=-312">What was here in 312 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
